---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas28.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Significant Influence — The 20% Threshold" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Equity Method — Initial & Subsequent Measurement" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Losses Exceeding the Investment" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Impairment of Equity-Accounted Investments" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Indian Conglomerate & Infrastructure JV Context" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Discontinuing the Equity Method" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 28 vs IAS 28 — Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINKAGE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical equity method philosophy to IAS 28</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 28 applies the IDENTICAL principle as IAS 28 — investments in associates (SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE, not control) and joint ventures (JOINT CONTROL, classified as a JV under Ind AS 111) are accounted for using the EQUITY METHOD, recognising the investor's SHARE of the investee's profit/loss.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 28 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list"><span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> — the equity method reflects the investor's economic interest in the investee's net assets/performance more faithfully than a simple cost-based approach — identical to IAS 28</span><span><strong>Widespread Indian application</strong> — Indian business groups (conglomerates) and infrastructure companies FREQUENTLY hold associate/JV stakes as part of their corporate structuring, making Ind AS 28's equity method GENUINELY significant in Indian practice</span></div>
        <div class="note">Ind AS 28 introduced NO substantive change to the equity method relative to IAS 28 — its Indian relevance is most pronounced for the SCALE and FREQUENCY of associate/JV holdings among Indian business groups.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">The significant influence presumption — identical to IAS 28</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><div class="f-eq">Holding ≥ 20% of voting power = REBUTTABLE presumption of significant influence</div></div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">ℹ️ IDENTICAL threshold and rebuttable nature to IAS 28.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & APPLICABILITY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw"><div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 28 covers</div><div class="principle">Ind AS 28 applies to accounting for investments in associates and joint ventures, and prescribes the equity method's application — IDENTICAL scope to IAS 28.</div></div>
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Significant influence</div><div class="glossary-def">IDENTICAL definition to IAS 28 — the power to participate in financial/operating policy decisions, without control or joint control.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Equity method</div><div class="glossary-def">IDENTICAL definition to IAS 28 — initial recognition at cost, adjusted for the investor's post-acquisition share of the investee's profit/loss and OCI.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Significant Influence — The 20% Threshold (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical rebuttable presumption to IAS 28</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 1: Significant influence despite a sub-20% holding</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian company holds only 15% of an investee's voting shares but has a contractual right to appoint ONE director to the investee's board and participates in key policy-making decisions.</div><div class="wk-answer">Despite the SUB-20% holding, significant influence likely EXISTS due to board representation and participation in policy decisions — an IDENTICAL rebuttable presumption analysis to how IAS 28 would assess the same fact pattern globally.</div></div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — THE EQUITY METHOD ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Equity Method — Initial &amp; Subsequent Measurement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Identical mechanics to IAS 28</div><div class="text-supporting">Initial recognition at cost; subsequently ADJUSTED for the investor's share of profit/loss (through P&amp;L) and OCI (through OCI), with dividends received REDUCING the carrying amount — IDENTICAL to IAS 28.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — LOSSES EXCEEDING THE INVESTMENT ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Losses Exceeding the Investment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical to IAS 28</div><div class="text-supporting">Once the investment's carrying amount reaches ZERO, further losses are recognised ONLY to the extent the investor has incurred legal/constructive obligations or made payments on the investee's behalf — IDENTICAL to IAS 28.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — IMPAIRMENT ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Impairment of Equity-Accounted Investments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 28</div><div class="text-supporting">The ENTIRE carrying amount of the investment (not underlying assets separately) is tested for impairment under Ind AS 36's recoverable amount framework when indicators exist — IDENTICAL to IAS 28.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — INDIAN CONTEXT ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Indian Conglomerate &amp; Infrastructure JV Context</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A practically prominent Indian application area</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Large Indian business groups frequently structure cross-holdings, strategic partnerships, and infrastructure/manufacturing joint ventures involving SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE (rather than control) stakes — making Ind AS 28's equity method GENUINELY significant across sectors including telecom, cement, automotive, and financial services, where minority strategic stakes and joint venture structures (often with foreign partners) are common.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — DISCONTINUING THE EQUITY METHOD ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Discontinuing the Equity Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Identical to IAS 28</div><div class="text-supporting">The equity method ceases from the date significant influence/joint control is LOST, with the RETAINED interest remeasured at fair value — IDENTICAL to IAS 28.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical disclosure requirements to IAS 28</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature/extent of significant restrictions on transferring funds</span><span>Unrecognised share of losses of an associate/JV</span><span>Summarised financial information (cross-referenced from Ind AS 112)</span></div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether significant influence exists at sub-20% holdings, or is rebutted above 20%</span><span>Whether losses continue to be recognised once the investment reaches zero</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Recoverable amount for impairment testing</span><span>Fair value of a retained interest on loss of significant influence</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">⚠️ Indian conglomerate cross-holding structures sometimes require careful analysis of AGGREGATE influence (direct plus indirect through other group entities) to correctly determine significant influence.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IND AS 28 vs IAS 28 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 28 vs IAS 28 — Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IAS 28</span><span>Ind AS 28</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Equity method mechanics, significant influence threshold</span><span>Standard model</span><span>IDENTICAL — no substantive divergence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Practical application context</span><span>Varies by jurisdiction</span><span>Especially prominent given widespread Indian conglomerate cross-holdings and infrastructure JV structures</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Ind AS 28 is essentially IDENTICAL to IAS 28 in substance — its distinctive Indian relevance lies in the SCALE and FREQUENCY of associate/JV structures among Indian business groups, not any accounting divergence.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Share of associate/JV profit or loss</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Identical to IAS 28</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Share of associate/JV OCI</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span></span><span>Identical to IAS 28</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Carrying amount of the investment</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Single-line equity-accounted presentation</span></div>
      </div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 28</span><span>The global counterpart standard, near-identically converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 111</span><span>Determines whether a joint arrangement qualifies as a JV subject to Ind AS 28's equity method</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 36</span><span>Governs impairment testing of the equity-accounted investment's carrying amount</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Significant influence</div><div class="glossary-def">Power to participate in financial/operating policy decisions without control — identical to IAS 28.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Rebuttable presumption</div><div class="glossary-def">The 20% voting power threshold creates a presumption of significant influence that can be overcome with clear contrary evidence.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Significant influence", definition: "The power to participate in financial/operating policy decisions without control or joint control — identical to IAS 28." },
      { term: "Equity method", definition: "Initial recognition at cost, adjusted for the investor's post-acquisition share of the investee's profit/loss and OCI — identical to IAS 28." },
      { term: "Rebuttable presumption", definition: "The 20% voting power threshold creates a presumption of significant influence that can be overcome with clear contrary evidence." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
